---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';
---

<Base title="How this website is built" description="How mxsalata.com is built: static pages, no trackers or cookies, and no inbound connections to the home network.">
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="eyebrow">Colophon</p>
    <h1>How this website is built</h1>
    <p class="lead">Small, fast and private by design.</p>
  </div>
  <article class="paper">
    <div class="prose">
      <section class="sect">
      <h2>Static pages at the edge</h2>
      <p>
        The site is plain HTML and CSS, built with <a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a> and served by Cloudflare.
        Its <a href={`${SITE.github}/site`}>source is on GitHub</a>; each change goes through a pull request, and the build
        and its checks run before anything is published.
      </p>
      </section>
      <section class="sect">
      <h2>Nothing extra in your browser</h2>
      <p>
        No trackers, no analytics and no cookies. When a page first loads, everything comes from this site: no fonts,
        scripts or images from elsewhere. The site's two small scripts are the theme switch, which remembers your choice on your own device, and
        "Book a meeting", which opens the Cal.com calendar in a window over the page. Cal.com loads only when you ask to
        book, and runs in its own frame under its own privacy policy.
      </p>
      </section>
      <section class="sect">
      <h2>Light, Pastel and Dark</h2>
      <p>
        The themes come from <a href="/projects/aperture/">Aperture</a>, so the two read as one family. Auto follows your
        device's setting.
      </p>
      </section>
      <section class="sect">
      <h2>How it protects itself, and you</h2>
      <p>
        Every page carries a restrictive content security policy: the browser may run only this site's own scripts and
        load its own styles and images, and the only outside frame it allows is Cal.com's, for booking. That limits what an
        injected script or a borrowed image could do. Pages are served only over HTTPS.
      </p>
      <p>
        Nothing private lives in the site. Its source is public, so the members' area for friends is a separate,
        private service behind Cloudflare Access: a friend signs in with a one-time code sent to their email, and only
        the addresses on the friends' list can sign in. Photos are stripped of their hidden details, such as where they were taken, before they're published.
      </p>
      <p>
        The home network accepts no inbound connections from the internet. What friends need, and the public Shutter
        demo, are reached through connections that start from home.
      </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>
